NEW[7] = [
('H2', '7.7 Heterojunctions, the HBT, and trapped charge in GaN'),
('P', "A heterojunction joins two crystals with different bandgaps whose lattices match closely enough to grow one on the other: AlGaAs on GaAs, InGaP on GaAs, InAlAs and InGaAs on InP, AlGaN on GaN, and SiGe on silicon. The gap difference splits into a step in the conduction band, ΔEc, and a step in the valence band, ΔEv. Chapter 1's bands now have corners, and devices are built by placing those corners. In a HEMT the conduction-band step holds electrons in the narrow-gap channel next to the interface, away from the dopants or polarization charge that supply them: the two-dimensional electron gas of Section 7.6."),
('P', "In a heterojunction bipolar transistor the emitter is the wide-gap side. Electrons flowing from emitter into base see the same barrier as in a silicon transistor, but holes flowing back from base into emitter see an extra step ΔEv, so the back-injected hole current, the base current of Chapter 8, falls by about exp(ΔEv/kT). The designer spends that factor less on β itself than on a base doped far more heavily than a homojunction would allow, which lowers the base resistance and permits a thin base with a short transit time. That trade is why the GaAs HBT is the handset power amplifier and why the InP HBT reaches hundreds of gigahertz. The SiGe HBT does the same inside a silicon process: germanium narrows the gap of the base, and grading the germanium across the base adds a built-in field that speeds electrons through it. Chapter 25 places SiGe among the other RF technologies."),
('EQ', 'hole back-injection reduced by ≈ exp(ΔEv / kT)'),
('WE', 'Worked Example 7.3', "A wide-gap emitter adds a valence-band step of 0.20 eV (an example value) against hole back-injection. By what factor does the hole current fall at room temperature? If the designer keeps β at 100, roughly how much more heavily may the base be doped than in a homojunction transistor?",
 "With kT = 26 meV the factor is exp(0.20/0.026) = exp(7.69) = 2,190. In a homojunction transistor β scales with the ratio of emitter to base doping; with the hole current suppressed 2,190 times, the base doping can rise by roughly that factor for the same β. Real HBTs dope the base one to two orders of magnitude above the emitter and still reach β near 100, with the rest of the margin spent on recombination and on grading the junction. The base resistance falls in proportion to the doping, which raises fmax and lowers noise."),
('P', "GaN has a problem of its own: trapped charge. Surface states between gate and drain, and deep levels in the buffer layer under the channel, capture electrons when the drain swings to high voltage and release them slowly, over microseconds to seconds. While they are full they deplete part of the two-dimensional gas. The result is current collapse, also called dynamic on-resistance: right after a high-voltage off state the device conducts less than its DC curves promise, and the knee of the RF load line walks out. Silicon nitride passivation, field plates that spread the gate-drain field, and carefully chosen carbon or iron doping in the buffer reduce the effect. Pulsed I-V measurements, which apply the drain voltage briefly from a stated quiescent point, expose it; a DC curve tracer does not."),
('WE', 'Worked Example 7.4', "A GaN HEMT on a 28 V supply has a 3 V knee and 1 A/mm in DC measurement. After a high-voltage pulse, trapped charge removes part of the sheet charge in the access region; assume this raises the knee to 6 V and lowers the maximum current to 0.8 A/mm. Compare the Class A power density before and after, using the estimate of Section 7.6.",
 "Before: (56 − 3) × 1/8 = 6.6 W/mm. After: (56 − 6) × 0.8/8 = 5.0 W/mm, 24 % less. Dynamic on-resistance in a power converter is the same mechanism read at a different terminal: if 20 % of the access-region charge is trapped, the access resistance rises by about 1/0.8 = 1.25, so a switch specified at 50 mΩ conducts like 62 mΩ for a while after each turn-off. Data sheets for GaN power transistors now state dynamic RDS(on), and RF foundries publish pulsed curves; use those, not the DC curves, in both designs."),
('KEY', "Key idea. A heterojunction is a designed band step. In a HEMT it confines the channel; in an HBT it blocks back-injection so that the base can be doped heavily. GaN's traps make its behavior depend on its recent history, which only pulsed measurements reveal."),
]
NEW[17] = [
('H2', '17.6 Hot carriers, oxide stress, and the voltage a chip may use'),
('P', "Two more aging mechanisms concern the transistor itself, and together they decide the supply voltage a process allows. Hot-carrier injection, HCI, happens near the drain of a conducting transistor. Electrons accelerated by the lateral field in the pinch-off region gain energies far above the thermal average, and a few of them create interface traps or are injected into the gate dielectric. The damage raises the threshold and lowers the drive current, and it grows roughly as a power of time, often near t^0.5 in published data, faster than the t^0.2 of NBTI. It is worst when the device carries current and sees a high drain voltage at the same time, which is why the transistors of an I/O driver, a power-amplifier stage, or a long-line driver age first. Lower supplies cut the peak field, part of the reason supplies fell from 5 V to below 1 V; the higher stress of RF and I/O circuits is handled with thick-oxide transistors, lightly doped drain extensions, and cascode stacks that share the voltage."),
('P', "Time-dependent dielectric breakdown, TDDB, is the oxide itself wearing out. Under a steady field, defects accumulate in the dielectric at random places until they link into a conducting path, first a soft breakdown that leaks, then a hard short. The time to breakdown is statistical, described by a Weibull distribution with a shape factor β, and it depends steeply on voltage. Reliability engineers stress small test capacitors at raised voltage and temperature until they fail, fit the distribution, and extrapolate in three steps: to the operating voltage, to the much larger gate area of a whole chip, and from the 63 % failure point to a failure fraction in parts per million."),
('EQ', 't(F) = t63 × [−ln(1 − F)]^(1/β),   t63(chip) = t63(test) × (Atest / Achip)^(1/β)'),
('WE', 'Worked Example 17.6', "Test capacitors of a thin gate oxide reach 63 % failures after 10 h at 2.2 V. Assume, as example values, that lifetime rises tenfold for every 0.1 V of lower stress, a Weibull shape factor β = 1.5, and a chip whose total gate area is 1,000 times that of the test capacitor. Find the time to 0.01 % chip failures at 1.8 V, and the highest voltage that gives ten years.",
 "At 1.8 V the test capacitor's t63 is 10 h × 10⁴ = 10⁵ h. Area scaling multiplies by (1/1000)^(1/1.5) = 0.01, giving 1,000 h for the chip. The 0.01 % point multiplies by (−ln(1 − 10⁻⁴))^(1/1.5) = (10⁻⁴)^0.667 = 2.15×10⁻³, so 0.01 % of the chips fail after about 2.2 h. Ten years is 87,600 h, which needs 87,600/2.15 = 4.07×10⁴ times more life, 4.61 decades, so the voltage must fall by 0.46 V to about 1.34 V (Figure 17.2). An oxide that survives 1.8 V for hours in a test supports little more than 1.3 V on a full chip for ten years, and that gap is why the published core supply of a process sits so far below the voltage its oxide survives in a quick test."),
('FIG', 'b3s_tddb.png', "Figure 17.2 — The extrapolation of Worked Example 17.6 with its example parameters: the test capacitor's 63 % life (dashed) moved to a whole chip's 0.01 % life (solid). Ten years needs about 1.34 V.", "Log plot of time against gate voltage with a dashed test-capacitor line and a solid chip line two and a half decades lower, crossing the ten-year line near 1.34 volts."),
('KEY', "Key idea. Hot carriers age the transistors that carry current at high drain voltage; TDDB wears out the gate oxide under any steady field. Both are extrapolated from short, harsh tests, and the extrapolation to a whole chip and a ppm failure rate is what sets the allowed supply voltage."),
]
