('B', "Thin-film lithium niobate, laterally excited: in the XBAR resonator of Plessky and colleagues (2019), a 400 nm Z-Y lithium niobate plate resonated near 4.8 GHz with a coupling of about 25 % and an 11 % resonance spacing, enough for the widest 5G bands."),
('P', "Figure 21.2 draws the same list as a map. Quartz trades coupling for stability; the bulk lithium crystals give moderate to high coupling on inexpensive wafers but drift; the films give bulk acoustic waves their high velocity and so their frequency range; thin-film lithium niobate gives the coupling the widest bands need. Temperature-compensated SAW adds a silicon dioxide overlayer whose velocity rises with temperature and cancels much of the substrate's drift, and multilayer SAW on a silicon support does the same while raising Q. The filters built from these materials are the subject of Books 4 and 7; the materials are this book's."),
